The availability of sensor-rich smart wearables opens up opportunities for highly interactive, collaborative human-robot teams. While recent advances in robotics make them suitable for complex tasks such as search and rescue, real-world scenarios still heavily rely on human input and decision-making. In this work, we propose ConCord, a human-in-the-loop system for cooperative search and exploration. In this work, we propose ConCord, a human-in-the-loop system for cooperative search and exploration. Our contributions are twofold: (1) we introduce an abstraction that treats humans as real-time agents within robotic workflows; and (2) we extend classical exploration algorithms (e.g., frontier-based exploration) to incorporate real-time awareness of human exploration progress, enabling a controllable trade-off between complementary (coverage-maximizing) and supplementary (human-aware) exploration. We evaluate ConCord in both simulated and real-world environments and show that it achieves significantly improved exploration efficiency while also enabling systematic correction of incomplete human search.